Meet the YozmaTech

YozmaTech isn’t just another tech company – we’re a global team of go-getters, innovators, and A-players helping startups and product companies scale smarter and faster. We build dedicated development teams across 10+ countries, creating strong, long-term partnerships based on trust, transparency, and real impact. Here, every idea counts. We value people who are proactive, open-minded, and ready to grow. If you’re passionate about building meaningful products and want to join a team that feels like family – you’ll feel right at home with us.

We are looking for a Senior Full-Stack Engineer who can independently own the migration of a legacy portal end-to-end. The engagement is short-term, for 3–4 months.

Our client a dedicated modernization track, not a feature squad. We're rebuilding a large EV-charging back-office portal from a legacy stack onto React 18 + TypeScript, and we want it done fast. You take a legacy screen and own the whole path to its replacement: reverse-engineering what the old flow actually does, designing and building the BFF endpoint behind it, and shipping the new page.
The Team
Small and lean – a couple of developers on the same migration, plus QA. Minimal process, minimal handoffs. There is no PM writing you specs for the screens you replace: the legacy system is the spec, and you're expected to read it

Key Requirements:
🔹 8+ years of frontend development in production environments;
🔹 5+ years with React (v18; v19 a bonus);
🔹 Strong TypeScript, JavaScript, SCSS/Sass;
🔹 5+ Java/Kotlin, Spring Boot codebase – reading it and building BFF endpoints in it (you won't own core backend services);
🔹 Redux Toolkit, Thunk, RTK Query;
🔹 MUI, Vite, CSS Modules, Storybook;
🔹 Proven full ownership of features and flows – you scope, decide and ship without being managed through it;
🔹 Ability to reverse-engineer an unfamiliar codebase: trace a flow end to end, read backend code and SQL, and find the hidden branch nobody remembers;
🔹 Comfortable working in a Java/Kotlin, Spring Boot codebase – reading it and building BFF endpoints in it (you won't own core backend services);
🔹 Solid understanding of frontend architecture, design patterns and performance at scale;
🔹 Experience building or maintaining a shared component library;
🔹 English – Upper-Intermediate or higher.

Will be plus:
🔹 Experience on a large legacy migration / modernization project;
🔹 Familiarity with CI/CD pipelines, Docker, deployment workflows;
🔹 UI testing frameworks;
🔹 Experience with AI coding agents / CLI tools (Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot) – we use them daily;

What you will do:
🔹 Own migrations end-to-end: legacy flow → API contract → BFF endpoint → new page;
🔹 Establish what a legacy screen really does – the undocumented branches, the permission gates, the edge cases operators rely on – by reading code and data, not by asking around;
🔹 Build and extend the BFF layer (Java/Kotlin + Spring Boot): endpoints, response shapes, permission gates;
🔹 Build and contribute reusable components to our shared UIkit library;
🔹 Make the architectural calls on your own pages, and review the other migration developers' work;

What you're walking into:
🔹 Two portals live side by side. New pages are feature-flag gated and embedded next to legacy screens in iframes; navigation, state and deep links cross that boundary via postMessage. Coexistence, not a clean rewrite;
🔹 The legacy system is the requirements doc. 10+ years of accumulated behaviour, largely undocumented, partly dead code. Distinguishing intended behaviour from accident is the daily job;
🔹 A monolith at the other end of your BFF. Its own conventions – failure bodies returned with HTTP 200, inconsistent payload shapes, queries older than the framework you're wrapping them in;
🔹 Multi-tenant and permission-dense. Every page, tab and dropdown is gated per service provider and per role. An empty filter is usually a 403, not a rendering bug;
🔹 Real scale in the data layer. Large grids, virtualized infinite selects, look-ahead pagination, async exports. Render performance is a design constraint, not a polish pass;
🔹 Parity with the screen you replace. Operators live in these pages all day; a dropped seconds field in a timestamp comes back as a bug report;
🔹 Debt in the new repo too. Pre-existing type errors, lint noise, half-migrated patterns. Leave things better; don't declare bankruptcy;

Interview stages:
🔹 HR-interview;
🔹 Technical interview with client;
🔹 Reference check;
🔹 Offer;

